    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here's a simple explanation of the stages of Mitosis: \n\nMitosis is a type of cell division in which a cell divides into two daughter cells that are genetically identical to the parent cell. The process consists of several stages: prophase, metaphase, anaphase, and telophase. Prophase involves chromosomes condensing, the nuclear envelope dissolving, and the development of the mitotic spindle. In metaphase, chromosomes line up at the center of the cell. \n\nDuring anaphase, sister chromatids separate, moving to opposite ends of the cell. Ultimately, telophase results in the formation of two daughter cells, each with a complete set of chromosomes. This process is crucial for growth, repair, and replacement of cells in multicellular organisms.",
      "source": "target",
